description:
calendar.rs:172-177, event.rs:274-279, event_notification.rs:103-108, participant_identity.rs:151-156 all use the same 'continue on non-string' pattern:

    for id_val in destroy_arr {
        let id_str = match id_val.as_str() {
            Some(s) => s.to_owned(),
            None => continue,
        };
        ...
    }

A destroy array entry that is not a string (number, null, object, etc.) is silently skipped. The id never appears in destroyed[], notDestroyed[], or any error response — the client gets no signal that part of its request was malformed.

RFC 8620 §3.6.1 establishes invalidArguments as the method-level error for argument-shape violations. §5.3 'destroy' is typed as Id[]. A non-string element is a type violation of the destroy argument.

Two reasonable behaviours:
1. Hard-fail the whole call with invalidArguments method-error citing 'destroy[N]'.
2. Soft-fail per-entry into notDestroyed with a SetErrorType::invalidProperties — but notDestroyed keys are Ids (strings), so this doesn't fit.

The clean fix is option 1: validate destroy is Vec<String> up front, return invalidArguments otherwise. Then the loop body can be infallible on the input shape.

Test gap: no test exercises malformed destroy arrays. Add a per-handler test sending destroy: [null] or destroy: [{}] and assert invalidArguments.

P3 because real clients won't send malformed destroy entries; defence-in-depth more than user-visible bug.
